  "text": "Solution 2 is wrong. It claims size 1 is impossible, but the trivial universe {0} is closed under options and disjunctive sum and has a one-element quotient. Its own size-2 example is also unsupported, so its answer of 1 cannot stand.\n\nSolutions 1 and 3 turn on whether 5 is realizable, and I can't settle that from first principles.\n\n- **Solution 1** says only 3 fails. Its argument for size 3 is weak: \"L + L = L\" is a normal-play fact, and it does not show anything about misère outcomes. Its constructions for sizes 4 and 6 are asserted, not checked.\n- **Solution 3** says 3 and 5 fail. Its \"systematic check\" of order 5 is asserted, not shown, and its size-4 example (subtract-2 giving Z/4) is not verified.\n\nI have a weak recollection from the partizan misère literature (Carvalho, Huggan, Nowakowski and Santos) that small odd orders are the obstructions, which leans toward 3 being the only exception, but I cannot confirm that. My confidence that Solution 1's answer of 3 is correct is low, and it is still the most plausible of the three.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
